{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KtsKf4TdvnzLgfZLTLXFSV","type":"tool_result","content":"CLAUDE.md\nDockerfile\nLICENSE\nMakefile\nREADME.md\ncmd\ndeploy\ndocs\ngo.mod\ngo.sum\ninternal\nmkdocs.yml\ninternal:\nagent\nagentjob\nbotcheck\nbudget\ncli\ncloseout\ncommitter\nconfig\nconfigcmd\nconfigsrc\ncontroller\ncostblock\ncosts\ndigest\ndocscheck\neffects\nexecutor\ngithub\ngitref\ngitx\ninitcmd\nintake\njanitor\nlabels\nlimits\nmerger\nmodelauth\npool\nprgate\nprwatch\nreaper\nredact\nrepopath\nreviewer\nreviewfix\nroles\nscanner\nservecmd\nstate\ntriage\nversioncheck\nworker\nwtlock\n\ninternal/cli:\napp.go\ncli_test.go\ncmd_config.go\ncmd_init.go\ncmd_labels.go\ncmd_rebuild.go\ncmd_serve.go\ncmd_version.go\ncommands.go\ncommands_test.go\nlog.go\nlog_test.go\nroot.go\nrun.go\n\ninternal/labels/labelsync:\ncmd.go\ncmd_test.go\nsync.go\nsync_test.go\ncat: main.go: No such file or directory\n2:// injected through Handlers; a nil field runs as a \"not implemented\"\n31:// Handlers holds one function per implemented command. A nil field makes\n33:type Handlers struct {\n78:\tHandlers Handlers\n153:\t\t\tif got := Run(tt.args, &stdout, &stderr, Handlers{}, \"dev\"); got != tt.want {\n172:\tif got := Run([]string{\"help\"}, &stdout, &stderr, Handlers{}, \"dev\"); got != 0 {\n186:func TestRunHandlers(t *testing.T) {\n190:\t\th := Handlers{\n217:\t\th := Handlers{\n235:\t\th := Handlers{\ncosts\nlogs\nplan.md\ntriage-engineer.md\ntriage-lead.md\n(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.go\n299:Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands; fetches, commits and pushes do not take it. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/84)), but a role that reads untrusted input (every triage, worker and reviewer role, and an LLM scanner) warns loudly when it has no `Dockerfile`; deterministic scanners are exempt, and nothing warns under `executor: k8s`, where every job runs in a container. `ard config check` and the first successful config load of `ard serve` (and any role a later config adds) log one `executor.host_untrusted` WARN per such role and also write it to stderr, so it survives `--log-level`. The local executor logs the WARN again for every host-mode job of such a role and appends a `host_untrusted` row to the `events` table. **Default agent image under the local executor (#244, part of #55).** `ard serve` and `ard config check` read the host setting `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`: a digest-pinned reference (`<name>@sha256:<64 hex>`) or a local image ID (`sha256:<hex>` from `make agent-image`); tags are refused (`executor.CheckImageRef`, `ErrImageRef`), and an invalid value stops startup. When it is set, `Prepare` runs every job that reads untrusted input and has no `Dockerfile` (triage, worker agent, test and fix steps, reviewer, and LLM scanner jobs) under Docker in that image, with the same flags and mounts as a Dockerfile role (`--cap-drop ALL`, `no-new-privileges`, worktree at `/work`, role directory `:ro`, and so on). A role's own `Dockerfile` wins. Deterministic scanners (trusted input) stay on the host. A local image is used as is; a digest that is not present is pulled. There is no fallback to host mode: `ard serve` runs `docker version` at startup and fails if Docker is down, and a job whose Docker setup fails at `Prepare` fails there. The controller's clone is not mounted, so `git` has no repository inside the container; every Docker job gets `GOFLAGS=-buildvcs=false` so `go build` does not fail on VCS stamping. Unset keeps today's behaviour. With it set, `ard config check` and `ard serve` no longer log or record `executor.host_untrusted` for roles it covers, and the hint of the warning names `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`. Each warning lists what the job can reach, including `~/.ard` (state, config snapshots, the pause file, other jobs' worktrees and logs). The owner accepts this risk while ard runs only on owner-controlled repos, and revisits it when moving to the spark cluster, where every job runs in a container once the k8s executor (#54) lands. Until then the Gate 1 dry-run soak on spark (see Rollout and Delivery) runs `ard serve` with the local executor in a pod, and the deterministic cve scanner runs there as a host subprocess of the controller container: its role directory has no `Dockerfile`, and the `ard` image carries its pinned tools. Only deterministic scanners run in that pod: it starts `ard serve --loops scanners,stale,reaper` ([#187](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/187)), so the triage, worker and reviewer loops never run there, even though `.ard/config.yaml` on `main` has agent roles. `--loops` takes a comma-separated list of loop names (scanners, stale, triage, workers, reviewers, reviewfix, merger, janitor, reaper, budget; `budget` is the status-issue notice loop, the claim gate is always on), defaults to every loop, and rejects an unknown name with exit 2. `ard serve` and `ard config check` refuse to run on git older than 2.47.0, because the committer's fetches fail on older versions.\n320:**Agent CLIs plug in through adapters.** Each adapter turns (prompt file, context paths, budget) into a command line and parses the exit code and, where the CLI reports them, turns and cost. For Claude Code that is `claude -p --output-format=stream-json --verbose --restricted --safe-mode --strict-mcp-config --disable-slash-commands --permission-mode=dontAsk --settings='{\"includeGitInstructions\":false,\"disableAllHooks\":true}' --tools=… --allowed-tools=… --disallowed-tools=… --model <model> --max-budget-usd <max_budget_usd> --append-system-prompt-file <role prompt> -- <message>` (`--verbose` is required by `stream-json` in print mode). Every stage (scanner, triage, worker, review) gets the same tool profile: `--tools` and `--allowed-tools` are `Read,Glob,Grep,Edit,Write,Bash`, and `--disallowed-tools` is `Bash(git commit *),Bash(git push *),Bash(git remote *),Bash(gh *)` (decided, #79). ard does not pass `--bare`: it limits the CLI to Bash, Read and Edit, and `--tools` cannot widen it (#105). The planted-config isolation `--bare` gave comes instead from `--restricted` (only managed settings and `--settings` load), `--safe-mode` (no CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md, skills, agents, hooks, MCP servers or auto memory), `--strict-mcp-config`, `--disable-slash-commands`, and `--settings` that turns off hooks and the git status snapshot (commit subjects are third-party text). `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_BACKGROUND_TASKS=1` is set as a fixed env var (the executor allows it) so Bash cannot detach commands. The integration tests pin each of these against a worktree with planted config and must be re-run on every `TestedVersion` bump. The tool list is not a security boundary, and the deny list is advisory, because shell indirection gets around it. The real boundaries are the missing GitHub token (Docker; see the host-mode note above, #84), the committer, which commits only `.ard/<issue#>/` from a triage or review job (other edits are discarded with the worktree), and the diff guard on worker changes. An explicit tool list from the caller (`Spec.Tools`) replaces the allow list and keeps the deny list. The user message holds only fixed text and the paths of the untrusted input files. The installed CLI accepts an undocumented `--max-turns`; ard does not rely on it, so a per-job dollar cap and the executor's hard timeout bound each run. Agents run with an empty per-job CLI config dir and HOME and receive exactly one model credential, passed by name by the executor and never visible to the adapter: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) or a subscription OAuth token (`CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`, from `claude setup-token`); that rests on the environment, not on a CLI flag. `ard serve` chooses the credential from its own environment: `ARD_AGENT_AUTH` is `auto` (the default; the token if set, else the key), `oauth` or `apikey`, and an explicit mode never falls back to the other variable. Only one is ever passed, because the CLI prefers the API key and would silently bill it (pinned by an integration test). A missing credential does not stop startup, because deterministic scanners need none: `ard serve` logs one INFO line, and a job that needs one fails with a clear error. The executor creates the per-job config dir as `<job home>/.claude` (0700) in `prepare` (`Handle.View.ConfigDir`; under Docker it is a bind mount at `/ard-config`), refuses to start a job whose config dir is not that empty directory, and removes it with the job home; the Reaper's `KeepWorktree` retention keeps it too, and it holds no credential, since the credential travels only in the environment. `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBPROCESS_ENV_SCRUB=1`, which would keep the token out of Bash children, is not set (decided, #125). Residual risk: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) is inherited by Bash children; on the tested release the CLI does not pass `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` to them; and either credential can still be read from the `claude` process's own environment (`/proc/$PPID/environ` on Linux, `ps eww` on macOS). The container is the boundary: it holds no GitHub token, and the credential is scoped to the model. The scrub is off because on 2.1.328 it requires bubblewrap (and socat) plus unprivileged user namespaces; Docker blocks those under ard's flags (`--cap-drop ALL`, `no-new-privileges`, default seccomp), so every Bash call would fail closed and without bwrap the CLI refuses to start. On macOS host mode it worked and matched the `dontAsk` outcomes. It can be revisited for host mode or a loosened container. What agent Bash can read differs by mode: under Docker, only the job's own credential; in host mode, far more, because any same-uid process's environment is readable (`ps eww <pid>` on macOS, `/proc/<pid>/environ` on Linux), including `ard serve`'s own, which holds both credentials. The scrub would not close that; it is part of the host-mode risk of #84. `kimi` and `dsh-tui` both run headless with `-p`; neither has a turn cap and `kimi` reports no cost, so turns and cost are optional in the adapter result. `config.yaml` names the default CLI and any per-role override, so a role can move between CLIs without touching its prompt. The Claude adapter also reads a usage limit and token usage from the stream: a limit comes from a `rate_limit_event` with status `rejected` (its `resetsAt` is the reset time), from the CLI's own error text (`You've hit your session, weekly, Opus or Sonnet limit`) and from `rate_limit` or `billing_error` errors, and it records `usage` and `modelUsage` token counts (by input, output, cache write and cache read; per model when the result has `modelUsage`, which is preferred for accounting). That text is matched only on paths the CLI itself writes (an assistant message carrying an `error`, or an errored result with HTTP 429 or `api_error`), never in agent output, because an agent can be made to print the phrase. Only the job's own agent step and its fix steps are read for a limit: a worker's test step runs agent-written code, so its output is never classified (a printed limit line there cannot block the account). With an API key a 429 stays a transient `rate_limit` retry; only subscription (OAuth) runs have usage limits. The adapter also reads `rate_limit_info.unifiedWindows` from every `rate_limit_event` into `Result.Windows` (one reading per window type, the last winning, also when the run ends with no result). The controller records the readings for OAuth steps only, from the job's own agent and fix steps and never from a test step, including killed steps with no result; it logs them as `limits.reading`. A recording error is logged as `limits.observe_failed` and never fails the job. Nothing is gated on the readings yet (#63). The committed fixtures are scrubbed real captures where a human has made them (`testdata/CAPTURE.md`) and clearly named `synthetic_*` files elsewhere.\n503:**`ard init` creates and upgrades `.ard/` (decided, #27; revised, #303).** It builds `config.yaml` and the starter roles in memory (today `scanners/cve`, `scanners/lts`, `scanners/docs`, `scanners/error-codes`, `scanners/lint`, `scanners/missing-tests`, `scanners/trufflehog`, `scanners/trufflehog-history`, `triage/lead`, `triage/engineer`, `workers/engineer`, `reviewers/eng-review`, `reviewers/license-review`, `reviewers/product-manager-review` and `reviewers/security-review`; `intake: {mode: label}`, the protected-path defaults above, and the cve, lts, docs, error-codes, lint, lead, engineer, worker, eng-review, security-review, license-review and product-manager-review overrides), then brings `.ard/` up to them. By default it writes the **local working tree** for a human to commit; with `--pr` it commits them on a new branch `ard-init` cut from `main` through the effect layer and a narrow committer lane, pushes it and opens a PR, as before. Either way a human commits or merges: ard never writes `main`, and `scan` is written `100755`. **Upgrades** are tracked in `.ard/ard-init.lock`, a YAML file holding the sha256 of every file init created or updated; init adds or refreshes a file's entry whenever it writes that file. The user may edit the lock; ard uses it only to decide whether a file changed since init wrote it. Per file: absent, create it; identical to the new starter, leave it; hash equal to its lock entry, update it; anything else (including no lock or no entry) counts as edited and is never overwritten: init lists it with the starter's diff. A file the starter dropped is listed, never deleted, and init writes nothing outside `.ard/`. The three repo context templates (`.ard/PRODUCT.md`, `.ard/VISION.md`, `.ard/TECH_STACK.md`) are create-if-absent: written only when missing, never treated as an upgrade and never overwritten; the next steps tell an adopter with an existing config to add their paths to `merge.protected_paths`. In local mode init refuses while `.ard/` has uncommitted changes; in `--pr` mode it refuses while `ard-init` exists on the remote, whatever its PR's state, so it never force-pushes over or stacks commits on a human's edits. If nothing differs it prints `nothing to change`. Init runs the shared permission check (#302: repo reachable, App installed, the permissions ard needs) and reports it; a failed check warns in local mode and fails in `--pr` mode. It no longer syncs labels: that moved to `ard setup`. With `--dry-run` it prints the plan and writes nothing (local mode) or logs the commit, push and PR to the ledger (`--pr`). It ends by naming the next steps: commit, merge with **Squash and merge** or **Create a merge commit** (never a fast-forward from the command line, which would put the bot's commit on main's first-parent history in `--pr` mode; **Rebase and merge** is also safe, #85), then `ard setup`. When the lint scanner is among the files, it also says the scanner fails until `.ard/scanners/lint/baseline` is committed. Until #344 and #345 land, `ard init` behaves as before: PR only, conflicts unless `--force`, label sync first.\n505:**`ard setup` applies `.ard/` to GitHub (decided, #303).** It reads the local `./.ard/` and warns when it differs from `origin/main`, which `ard serve` runs. With no flags it runs every check, then applies only if all of them are green; `--check` reports only (exit 2 on drift) and `--dry-run` routes writes through the effect layer. Each row is `ok`, `changed`, `drift` or `manual` with the exact fix. It **writes** only two things: the `ard:*` label catalogue (create and update, never delete; `ard labels sync` stays as an alias) and the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN`, derived from the App's own identity (`<slug>[bot]`), never from config. Variables need the operator's token (`gh auth token` or a PAT, #302), read once, held in memory and never logged, stored or passed to an agent or job. It **only checks**, against a policy built into ard rather than repo content, and prints the fix on drift: the shared permission check; `main`'s active ruleset requires a pull request and its bypass list is exactly the ard App in mode Always; the App has no `workflows` or `administration` permission; merge settings (`allow_rebase_merge` off once versioning is on); and `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` on `origin/main` against a copy embedded in ard (`ard setup --print-workflow` prints it for a human to commit). Setup never commits to the repository, `.github/` included, and never writes rulesets, bypass actors or App settings: a merged config change must not be able to loosen branch protection through an admin token. `ard setup --create-gh-app` (#350) creates the App itself with GitHub's manifest flow.\n523:- **Planted repo config does not steer agents.** A branch can carry CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, `.claude/` settings, hooks, skills, agents or a `.mcp.json`. The Claude adapter's flags stop all of them loading (see Executors). Residual risk: auth isolation comes from the empty per-job config dir and HOME, not a flag (the CLI reads no Keychain entry but the one keyed to the per-job dir); a host-mode job on macOS still runs as the operator's uid, so agent Bash could call `security` against the operator's login Keychain, including Claude's own entry, which is within the host-mode risk of #84 (a Dockerfile role or a dedicated macOS user for `ard serve` closes it); the subscription token is a one-year, model-only credential, rotated by running `claude setup-token` again and restarting `ard serve`; `--max-budget-usd` and `total_cost_usd` are estimates on a subscription; part of the block (AGENTS.md, and CLAUDE.md under `--restricted`) is observed behaviour that holds only on the tested CLI version and is re-checked by the integration tests; built-in plugins and the host's managed settings still apply; system reminders and fixed CLI prompt text are back; isolation of settings `env` is not pinned by a test, since no Bash call is scripted; Bash can still read anything the process can (host mode, #84); and an agent can rewrite CLAUDE.md or `.claude/` in its worktree, which later runs ignore but a worker PR could carry into the target repo (the `ard init` protected-path defaults cover `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `.claude/` and `.mcp.json`, so such a PR needs a human).\n602:- [x] **`ard init` and `ard setup` (#303).** Decided 2026-11-24: `init` owns the content of `.ard/` (local tree by default, `--pr` for the `ard-init` PR, upgrades tracked in the user-editable `.ard/ard-init.lock`), and `setup` applies the local `.ard/` to GitHub, writing only `ard:*` labels and `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` and only checking rulesets, the bypass list, merge settings, App permissions and the bot-commits workflow. See the two paragraphs under Configuration. Implemented by #