ecks**, against a policy built into ard rather than repo content, and prints the fix on drift: the shared permission check; `main`'s active ruleset requires a pull request and its bypass list is exactly the ard App in mode Always; the App has no `workflows` or `administration` permission; merge settings (`allow_rebase_merge` off once versioning is on); and `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` on `origin/main` against a copy embedded in ard (`ard setup --print-workflow` prints it for a human to commit). Setup never commits to the repository, `.github/` included, and never writes rulesets, bypass actors or App settings: a merged config change must not be able to loosen branch protection through an admin token. `ard setup --create-gh-app` (#350) creates the App itself with GitHub's manifest flow.\ndocs/proposal.md:521:- **Intake is configurable (decided, #2).** `intake` is either `all` (every human-filed issue enters, no label) or `label` (only issues with the configured label). Issue templates and forms apply their labels on the author's behalf, so an outsider can file an issue that already carries the intake label. That is why, in label mode, the controller honours the label only if the latest `labeled` event came from an actor with triage or higher permission, or from the App; otherwise it adds `ard:needs-human`. The intake label must start with `ard:`, and a label ard itself applies (`ard:needs-human`, a stage label and so on) cannot be the intake label; `ard config check` and `ard labels sync` reject it, and `ard serve` never admits through it. In both modes the issue is re-gated if its body is edited afterwards. Note that `all` mode lets any issue author start a job, so use it only on repos where that is acceptable. **The one exception to \"the issue body is human-owned untrusted input\" is ard's cost block.** ard owns only the text between its `ard:cost` markers. The admission hash (`agentjob.BodyHash`) and the `issue.md` snapshot given to agents both use the body with that block stripped (`costblock.Strip`), so whatever the hash ignores is exactly what no agent sees: text hidden from the re-gate can never reach an agent. Strip removes only the last begin-to-end block, and only when its contents parse as exactly the table ard renders (fixed header, rows of validated cells, a Total row, at most 5,000 bytes; LF or CRLF); a fake block, markers around prose, or an edited table is not stripped, so it stays visible to agents and counts in the hash and an edit re-gates as usual. A body with no valid block hashes exactly as before, so stored admissions stay valid. ard never overwrites or drops such human text: it appends its own block after it. The block's header says so (\"do not edit: an edited block is kept as text, shown to agents and re-gates the issue\"). ard's body writes bump `updated_at`.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01APTtkV5giQXoedh2irV5Tj","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
